.TH _PRINTF 3 "2025-10-02" "v1.0" "Holberton School"

.SH NAME
_printf \- build new printf called "_printf"

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.br
.B int _printf(const char *format, ...);
.br
.B int _putchar(char c);
.br
.B int print_number(va_list ap);
.br
.B int print_unsigned(va_list ap, char spec);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The _printf function prints text to the screen. It can print characters, strings, and numbers.
It reads a format string and replaces special codes with the values we give.
It is kind of like the normal printf in C but simpler.

It handles these special codes:
.IP %c
Prints a single character.
.IP %s
Prints a string.
.IP %%
Prints a percent sign.
.IP %d
Prints a signed decimal integer.
.IP %i
Prints a signed decimal integer.
.IP %b
Prints an unsigned integer in binary (custom specifier).
.IP %u
Prints an unsigned decimal integer.
.IP %o
Prints an unsigned octal integer.
.IP %x
Prints an unsigned hexadecimal integer (lowercase).
.IP %X
Prints an unsigned hexadecimal integer (uppercase).

.SH RETURN VALUE
If everything works fine, _printf returns the number of characters printed.
If something goes wrong, it returns -1.

.SH EXAMPLES
To print a simple string:
.EX
_printf("Hello, world!\n");
.EE

To print a character and a string:
.EX
_printf("Character: %c, String: %s\n", 'A', "Test");
.EE

To print a percent sign:
.EX
_printf("Progress: 100%% complete\n");
.EE

To print a number:
.EX
_printf("Number: %d\n", 12345);
.EE

To print binary:
.EX
_printf("Binary: %b\n", 98);
.EE

To print unsigned, octal, and hex:
.EX
_printf("Unsigned: %u, Octal: %o, Hex: %x, HEX: %X\n", 255, 255, 255, 255);
.EE

